Problem 8

Protons are made up of smaller so-called "partons". The European Large Hadron Collider (LHC) is a high-energy proton–proton collider; the energy of a single proton in a proton beam is $E=7.0\ \mathrm{TeV}$ ($1\ \mathrm{TeV}=10^{3}\ \mathrm{GeV}=10^{12}\ \mathrm{eV}$). Two proton beams of equal energy move towards each other and collide head-on and break up, and two colliding partons a and b among them interact and annihilate, producing a new particle. Let the ratios of the kinetic energies of partons a and b to the proton energy be $x_{\mathrm{a}}$ and $x_{\mathrm{b}}$ respectively, the kinetic energies being much larger than their rest energies.

(1) Assuming that the collision and annihilation of the two partons a and b produce a new particle S of rest mass $m_{\mathrm{s}}=1.0\ \mathrm{TeV/c^{2}}$, find the product $x_{\mathrm{a}}x_{\mathrm{b}}$ of $x_{\mathrm{a}}$ and $x_{\mathrm{b}}$;

(2) Assuming that after being produced the new particle S decays into two photons, find the frequencies of the two photons in the reference frame in which the new particle S is at rest;

(3) Assuming that after being produced the new particle S decays, in its rest frame, into two light particles A of mass $m_{\mathrm{A}}=1.0\ \mathrm{GeV/c^{2}}$, and each light particle A in turn decays into two photons of equal frequency, find the angle between the momenta of these two photons in this frame.

Given: $\sin\alpha\approx\alpha$ when $\alpha\ll1$; Planck's constant $h=6.63\times10^{-34}\ \mathrm{J\cdot s}$; the magnitude of the electron charge $e=1.60\times10^{-19}\ \mathrm{C}$.

Problem 2

Binary star systems are an important class of objects of astronomical observation. Suppose two stars can both be regarded as point masses, with masses $M$ and $m$ respectively, moving together in circular orbits about their centre of mass and forming a binary system; the observed period of revolution of the system is $T_0$. At a certain moment the star $M$ suddenly explodes and loses mass $\Delta M$. Assume that the explosion is instantaneous and isotropic relative to the star $M$, so that the velocity of the remnant $M'$ ($M'=M-\Delta M$) of the star $M$ just after the explosion is the same as the velocity of the star $M$ just before the explosion, and that neither the explosion process nor the ejected matter $\Delta M$ has any effect on the star $m$. The gravitational constant $G$ is known; relativistic effects are not considered.

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Eisatopon/physics-olympiads-bank/main/images/cpho-2017-1-2-fig1.png

(1) Find the distance $r_0$ between the star $M$ and the star $m$ before the explosion;

(2) If after the explosion the star $M'$ and the star $m$ still move periodically, find the period $T_1$ of this motion;

(3) If after the explosion the star $M'$ and the star $m$ eventually separate forever, find the condition that $M$, $m$ and $\Delta M$ must satisfy.

Problem 4

As shown in the figure, in a uniform magnetic field of magnitude $B$ directed vertically upward there is a homogeneous, rigid, conducting square wire frame abcd of mass $m$, side length $l$ and total resistance $R$. The frame can rotate about a smooth axis $\rm OO'$ passing through the midpoints of sides ad and bc. P and Q are the two ends of the leads of the frame. The axis $\rm OO'$ and the X axis lie in the same horizontal plane and are perpendicular to each other. The self-inductance of the frame is not considered.

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Eisatopon/physics-olympiads-bank/main/images/cpho-2017-1-4-fig1.png

(1) Find the moment of inertia $J$ of the frame about the axis $\rm OO'$;

(2) At $t=0$ the frame is at rest, and its plane makes a very small angle $\theta_0$ with the X axis. At this moment a constant direct current of magnitude $I$ is passed through the frame, in the direction $\rm P\to a\to b\to c\to d\to Q$. Find the expressions for the time dependence of the angle $\theta$ between the plane of the frame and the X axis, of the angular velocity $\dot{\theta}$ and of the angular acceleration $\ddot{\theta}$ of the frame thereafter;

(3) At $t=t_0>0$ the plane of the frame has just rotated counterclockwise to the horizontal position; at this moment P and Q are disconnected from the external circuit. How does the frame move thereafter? Find the expression for the time dependence of the voltage $V_{\rm PQ}$ between P and Q;

(4) After the frame has moved as described above for some time, when the angle between its plane and the X axis is $\theta_1$ ($\frac{\pi}{4}\le\theta_1\le\frac{3\pi}{4}$), P and Q are short-circuited, and the frame rotates through a further small angle $\alpha$ and then stops. Find the relation between $\alpha$ and $\theta_1$, and the minimum value of $\alpha$.

Problem 6

In 1914, Franck and Hertz used the method of electron collisions with atoms to excite atoms from lower energy levels to higher energy levels, thereby proving the existence of atomic energy levels. Accelerated electrons collide with free hydrogen atoms, exciting a certain hydrogen atom from the ground state to an excited state. This hydrogen atom can emit only one spectral line within the range of visible wavelengths ($400\ {\rm nm}\sim760\ {\rm nm}$). Consider only one-dimensional head-on collisions.

(1) Find the wavelength of the visible light that this hydrogen atom can emit;

(2) Find the range of the kinetic energy $E_{\rm k}$ of the electron after acceleration;

(3) If the electron is replaced by a proton, find the range of the accelerating voltage for accelerating the proton.

Given: $hc=1240\ {\rm nm\cdot eV}$, where $h$ is Planck's constant and $c$ is the speed of light in vacuum; the mass of the proton is approximately 1836 times the mass of the electron; the velocity of the hydrogen atom before the collision may be neglected.

Problem 7

As shown in the gas pressure–volume ($P$-$V$) diagram, a system consisting of $\nu$ moles of a diatomic ideal gas undergoes a forward cyclic process (the forward cycle is the cycle in the direction of the arrows in the figure), in which the process from A to B is a straight-line process and the process from B to A is an isothermal process. The molar heat capacity at constant volume of a diatomic ideal gas is $\frac{5}{2}R$, where $R$ is the gas constant.

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Eisatopon/physics-olympiads-bank/main/images/cpho-2017-2-7-fig1.png

(1) Find the maximum temperature during the straight-line process AB;

(2) Find the expression for the molar heat capacity of the gas as a function of the gas volume during the straight-line process AB; state, for each range of volume of the straight-line process AB, whether the gas absorbs or releases heat; and determine the temperature $T_{\rm c}$ at which the change between heat absorption and heat release occurs;

(3) Find the net heat absorbed during the whole straight-line process AB, and the net work done by the gas on the surroundings in one forward cycle.

Problem 5

An adiabatic thin-walled container of mass $M$ is in outer space far from other celestial bodies (which may be regarded as a vacuum). Observed in a certain inertial frame, the initial velocity of the container is zero. The volume of the container is $V$, and the container is filled with a monatomic ideal gas of a certain kind; the initial number of gas molecules and the mass of a molecule are $N_0$ and $m$ respectively, and the initial temperature of the gas is $T_0$. At $t=0$ a small hole of area $S$ appears in the wall of the container; because of gas leaking through the small hole, the container starts to move, but the container does not rotate. Assume that the hole is rather small and that the gas in the container is always in equilibrium during the leakage. The Maxwell distribution function of the component $v_x$ of the molecular velocity along the $x$ direction is known to be $f(v_x)=\sqrt{\frac{m}{2\pi kT}}\exp\left(-\frac{mv_x^2}{2kT}\right)$ ($k$ is the Boltzmann constant). During the leakage, find:

(1) the number of gas molecules escaping through the small hole per unit time per unit area of the hole, when the number density of the gas molecules is $n$ and the temperature is $T$;

(2) the average kinetic energy, relative to the container, of the gas molecules escaping from the small hole when the gas temperature in the container is $T$;

(3) the temperature of the gas in the container at time $t$;

(4) the magnitude of the velocity of the container at time $t$ (assume $M\gg N_0 m$).

Known integral formulas: $\int_0^{\infty}x\mathrm{e}^{-Ax^2}\,\mathrm{d}x=\frac{1}{2A}$, $\int_0^{\infty}x^2\mathrm{e}^{-Ax^2}\,\mathrm{d}x=\frac{1}{4}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{A^3}}$, $\int_0^{\infty}x^3\mathrm{e}^{-Ax^2}\,\mathrm{d}x=\frac{1}{2A^2}$.

Problem 2

A half-cylinder of length $l$ with inner and outer radii $a$ and $b$ respectively is made of two different lossy dielectrics, whose relative permittivities and conductivities are respectively $\varepsilon_{r1}$ and $\sigma_1$ (region $0\le\varphi<\theta_0$) and $\varepsilon_{r2}$ and $\sigma_2$ (region $\theta_0<\varphi\le\pi$); its cross-section is shown in Fig. a. The bottoms of the two sides of the half-cylinder are coated with metal films; a DC voltage $V_0$ is applied between the two metal films and a steady state is reached. The vacuum permittivity is known to be $\varepsilon_0$; the relative permittivities of the two dielectrics are large and their conductivities are small. Neglect edge effects.

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Eisatopon/physics-olympiads-bank/main/images/cpho-2019-1-2-fig1.png

Fig. a

1) Find the distributions of the electric field strength and of the potential in the dielectrics;

2) find the total charge on the interface $\varphi=\theta_0$;

3) calculate the resistance and the capacitance of each of the two dielectric regions $0\le\varphi<\theta_0$ and $\theta_0<\varphi\le\pi$;

4) at the instant $t=0$ the power supply is disconnected; find how the voltage between the two metal films then varies with time (this process can be regarded as quasi-stationary), and draw the corresponding equivalent circuit.

Problem 3

A simplified model of an electromagnetic railgun is shown in Figure a. Two fixed cylindrical guide rails are parallel to each other; the plane containing their axes of symmetry makes an angle $\theta$ with the horizontal plane. Each of the two rails has length $L$, radius $b$ and resistance per unit length $\lambda$; the closest distance between the two rails is $d$ ($d$ is small). A metal projectile of mass $m$ ($m$ is rather small) (which may be regarded as a thin sheet) is placed between the two rails; the diameter of the projectile is $d$ and its resistance is $R$, and it keeps good contact with the rails. The lower ends of the two rails have their cross-sections in a common plane; the lower ends are connected (through two rather long rigid wires, not drawn, coaxial with the corresponding rails and of the same cross-section) to an ideal constant-current source of current $I$ (the energy loss inside the constant-current source is negligible). Air resistance and friction are not considered; the magnitude of the gravitational acceleration is $g$ and the permeability of vacuum is $\mu_0$. Consider the process in which the projectile starts from rest at the lower end of the rails, is accelerated by the magnetic field, and is finally shot out.

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Eisatopon/physics-olympiads-bank/main/images/cpho-2019-2-3-fig1.png

Figure a. Simplified model of an electromagnetic railgun

[Labels in the figure: 导轨 = guide rail; 弹丸 = projectile; 恒流源 = constant-current source.]

1) Find the magnetic force acting on the projectile during the acceleration;

2) Find the exit velocity of the projectile;

3) Find the voltage across the terminals of the ideal constant-current source at an arbitrary moment during the acceleration of the projectile, and at the moment the projectile is shot out;

4) Find the work done by the ideal constant-current source during the whole acceleration of the projectile;

5) Under the condition $\theta = 0^\circ$, if the resistances of the rails and of the projectile are both negligible, find the ratio of the kinetic energy of the projectile when it is shot out to the work done by the ideal constant-current source.
